+    <section class="journal" aria-labelledby="journal-title">
+      <p class="journal-kicker">From origin to your morning</p>
+      <h2 id="journal-title">Roaster's Journal</h2>
+      <p class="journal-intro">Good coffee begins long before the first roast. Here’s how we follow the bean back to its roots.</p>
+
+      <div id="journal-story" class="journal-story">
+        <p>Every coffee we bring into The Daily Grind starts with a question: who grew it, and what made this harvest special? We look for coffees with a clear connection to their origin, from the farm or cooperative that raised the cherries to the mill that prepared them for export. Knowing that journey helps us understand more than a flavor profile. It gives us a way to recognize the people, the landscape, and the patient work behind every bag.</p>
+
+        <p>Our sourcing process begins with conversations with producers and importers who can share the details of a lot: where it was grown, when it was picked, how it was processed, and how it moved through the supply chain. We ask about buying terms and the challenges of the season, because quality has a cost that goes beyond the bean itself. Our aim is to build lasting relationships and make purchasing decisions that value the skill and care of the people growing our coffee.</p>
+
+        <p>Before choosing a coffee, we roast small samples and taste them side by side in a process called cupping. We pay attention to sweetness, acidity, body, and the finish that lingers after each sip. A bright, fruit-forward lot might be a beautiful choice for a filter brew, while a rounded coffee with cocoa notes might become the foundation of an espresso blend. We taste more than once, allowing the samples to cool so that subtle qualities have time to emerge. A compelling story matters to us, and so does a cup that lives up to it.</p>
+
+        <p>We also ask how the land is being cared for. Shade trees, soil health, water use, and the handling of processing waste are all part of the conversation. Conditions differ from one region to another, so we seek specific information rather than assuming one label tells the whole story. Coffee is a seasonal crop shaped by weather and changing growing conditions. Staying curious about those changes helps us choose thoughtfully and appreciate why the same origin can taste a little different from year to year.</p>
+
+        <p>Once a lot reaches our roastery, we develop a roast that brings its character into focus. We work in small batches, record each roast, and keep tasting as the coffee rests. Those notes guide adjustments to time and temperature, helping us preserve sweetness and balance instead of hiding the bean’s personality. The journey ends with a fresh bag and a cup in your hands, but our work continues with the next sample, the next conversation, and the next harvest. That ongoing attention is what makes sourcing part of our everyday craft.</p>
+      </div>
+
